==Behind the scenes==
{{Quote|Two Easter eggs that I suggested on the page got in. One was the name of the hospital that Kleya goes to, which is called Lina Soh. She was the Chancellor from the High Republic days. And I had no idea if it was gonna get in. I just stuck it into the script, and then someone went and named it that.|Tom Bissell|<ref name="Easter egg">{{WebCite|url=https://www.inverse.com/entertainment/andor-writer-interview-wheel-of-fate-easter-eggs|text=Andor's Writer Offers A Different Perspective On The Wheel of Fate|work=[[Wikipedia:Inverse (website)|Inverse]]|date=2025-06-01|archivedate=20250602174457}}</ref>}}
[[File:Lina_Soh_Hospital.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Lina Soh Hospital's interior]]
The Lina Soh Hospital first appeared in "[[Make It Stop]]," the tenth episode of the [[Andor Season 2|second season]] of the live-action television series [[Star Wars: Andor|''Andor'']],<ref name="Stop" /> which released on [[May 13]], [[2025]].<ref name="Trailer News">{{SW|url=news/andor-season-2-teaser-trailer-key-art-reveal|text=First ''Andor A Star Wars Story'' Teaser Trailer and Poster Revealed}}</ref> Series [[Wikipedia:Writer|writer]] [[Tom Bissell]] named the hospital after the High Republic chancellor as an easter egg, though initially unsure if the name was going to stick outside of the script.<ref name="Easter egg" /> The hospital set was a favorite of [[Wikipedia:Showrunner|showrunner]] [[Tony Gilroy]].<ref name="Trivia" /> The sign displaying Lina Soh's name uses the Aurebesh character for "1" instead of the "I" character.<ref name="Stop" /> ''Andor'' production designer [[Luke Hull]] considered creating the Coruscant hospital a challenge, and revealed that it was initially designed after the [[Wikipedia:Lloyd's building|Lloyd's building]] in [[Wikipedia:London|London]], with consideration even going into filming the scenes in empty floors of the Lloyd's building. However, Hull and set decorator [[Rebecca Alleway]] eventually settled on building a bright single u-shaped set that could be modified to depict different floors, taking inspiration from Japanese hospitals.<ref name="Worlds of Season 2">{{SW|url=news/andor-season-2-world-building-production-design-feature|text=The Secrets of Ghorman, Mina-Rau, and More: Creating the Worlds of Andor Season 2 - Updated}}</ref>

